-## Subtopics
-
-### Character SDFs
-Represents letters as line segments with a thickness offset. Chosen when rendering typographic elements as geometry rather than textures. [S1, unofficial]
-
-### Arc SDFs
-Represents curved segments. Chosen for circular or curved typographic elements. [S1, unofficial]
-
-### Adaptive Sampling
-Uses variance of luminance to stop sampling early. Chosen to reduce noise in smooth areas while spending more samples in noisy areas. [S1, unofficial]
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot M \cdot B)$, where $N$ is pixels, $M$ is samples per pixel (adaptive), $B$ is bounces, and each trace step involves iterating over all shapes/characters/arcs. The character check is $O(K)$ where $K$ is number of character pairs.
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(S + C + A)$ for storing shapes, characters, and arcs.
-*   **Performance**: The source reports rendering times on a 128-thread CPU [S1, unofficial]. The adaptive sampling reduces total samples for smooth regions.
-*   **Strengths**: Handles mixed geometry (primitives + text/arcs) in one pass. Adaptive sampling improves efficiency.
-*   **Limitations**: No spatial acceleration structure (BVH) for the SDFs themselves, only a bounding box check. Finite difference normals are noisy and expensive.
-*   **When to use**: Real-time or offline rendering of scenes with significant text/arc geometry where volumetric methods are overkill.
-*   **When not to use**: Scenes with millions of primitives (no BVH). High-precision normal requirements (finite differences are approximate).
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot M \cdot B)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels, $M$ is the number of samples per pixel (adaptive), $B$ is the max bounces (3), and the cost per bounce depends on the number of shapes, characters, and arcs checked. The character check is $O(K)$ where $K$ is the number of character pairs, only performed if near the bounding box [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(S + C + A)$, storing shapes, characters, and arcs [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The method uses adaptive sampling to reduce computation for converged pixels. The use of a surrounding bounding box accelerates character/arc checks by limiting them to rays near the geometry [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficiently renders complex text and arcs as SDFs. Adaptive sampling reduces noise.
+*   **Limitations:** Normal estimation for characters/arcs uses finite differences, which can be noisy or inaccurate if `smallStep` is not chosen carefully. The hit detection for characters is based on line segments, not true 3D extruded letters, which may cause visual artifacts if viewed from extreme angles or if the thickness offset is inappropriate [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Scenes with prominent text or arc-based geometry where SDF rendering is desired.
+*   **When not to use:** Scenes with high-frequency detail not representable by SDFs or where analytical normals are critical for high-quality reflections.
 
